WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:02.710
Hey guys, it's Jan here.

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:06.910
As we've come to expect from Marvel's productions,
there's a stack of easter eggs and references

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:11.240
in Daredevil Season 2 to the Marvel Cinematic
Universe -and the comic books.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:16.119
With all 13 episodes out now on Netflix, I've
put together all the easter eggs and references

00:00:16.119 --> 00:00:18.010
I could find in this video.

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:20.980
If you've spotted other ones too, do let me
know about them in the comments below!

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:25.130
Just before I kick off, I've got an awesome
giveaway from our friends at Entertainment

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:26.130
Earth.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:30.120
For a chance to win this cool set of Daredevil
collectibles, including a Daredevil action

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:35.321
figure, Daredevil watch, and Daredevil villain
Bullseye piece plus Collector magazine, make

00:00:35.321 --> 00:00:40.760
sure you subscribe and comment about your
favourite Season 2 easter egg on this video.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:45.170
For more details and ways to enter the comp,
check out the Gleam link in the video description

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:49.050
below and also the links to the prizes on
Entertainment Earth!

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.729
And just a quick reminder, there will be spoilers
ahead, so if you're not ready for that, check

00:00:52.729 --> 00:00:55.989
out my non-spoiler review of season 2 here!

00:00:55.989 --> 00:01:00.129
Kicking off with the costumes, this season
Daredevil gets various upgrades starting with

00:01:00.129 --> 00:01:04.239
the addition of some creepy red eyes that
recall the time in the Shadowland comics,

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:08.250
where Matt Murdock was corrupted by the Hand
and later possessed by the demon the ninja

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:09.560
clan serves.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:14.280
And Daredevil's costumer Melvin Potter also
gives him new billy clubs that double as grappling

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.439
hooks, just like Daredevil has in the comic
books.

00:01:17.439 --> 00:01:21.829
Elektra's costume isn't as revealing as she's
often dressed in the comics or when Jennifer

00:01:21.829 --> 00:01:26.350
Garner played her in the 2005 movie, but there
are nods to her classic red colours under

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.229
her more practical black gear.

00:01:28.229 --> 00:01:31.700
And it's interesting that after The Hand recovered
Elektra from her grave that she was clothed

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:34.869
in a red sash or dress that's much closer
to her comic book look.

00:01:34.869 --> 00:01:39.659
Elektra also got to wield her classic sai
from the comics after she took them from Jacques

00:01:39.659 --> 00:01:41.509
Duchamps who Stick sent to kill her.

00:01:41.509 --> 00:01:45.219
There were loads of teases to The Punisher's
iconic white skull from the comics throughout

00:01:45.219 --> 00:01:50.979
the episodes too, with x-rays of Frank Castle's
skull and when Castle spray-paints his armoured

00:01:50.979 --> 00:01:56.299
vest in the finale it's a reference to a draft
of the 1989 Punisher movie with Dolph Lundgren

00:01:56.299 --> 00:02:00.159
in which Castle spray painted the iconic skull
onto his Kevlar vest.

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:03.899
The scene was cut from that movie by a producer
as it was considered too comic-booky.

00:02:03.899 --> 00:02:08.710
But in Daredevil, we do get a proper shot
of Castle in his final uniform with skull

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:12.209
symbol clearly showing in that final rooftop
scene.

00:02:12.209 --> 00:02:16.440
Frank Castle's story of losing his wife and
children to the mob is an echo of his origin

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:20.120
story in the comics where his family was gunned
down in Central Park.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:24.620
The x-ray of Castle's skull also shows a dark
circle to the left and Karen mentions that

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:27.940
Frank was also hit in the head, a reference
to the bullet wound Frank Castle took to the

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:30.310
head in the comics during the mob attack.

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:34.290
Numerous other storylines from the Daredevil
and Punisher comic books have been adapted

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:35.290
for season 2.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:39.440
For example, the botched sting operation organised
by Reyes for which she decided not to clear

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.930
Central Park is a reference to the comic Punisher
Kills The Marvel Universe.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:47.920
In that comic, the Avengers and the X-Men
accidentally kill Frank Castle's family in

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.320
Central Park while thwarting an alien strike
force.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:54.739
Captain America admits they didn't have time
to clear the area.

00:02:54.739 --> 00:02:59.010
The scene in which Castle chains up Daredevil
on a rooftop is an homage to the Punisher

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:03.319
comic The Choice in which the Punisher is
about to assassinate the criminal Dino Gnucci,

00:03:03.319 --> 00:03:07.709
and he gives Daredevil a gun telling him the
only way to stop him shooting Gnucci is to

00:03:07.709 --> 00:03:09.400
kill the Punisher himself.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.790
The TV show finds an interesting resolution
with Matt shooting his chains off rather than

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:16.320
trying to shoot the Punisher as he does in
comics.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.299
The fight scene between the two which follows
also has shades of Daredevil #257 where Punisher

00:03:20.299 --> 00:03:26.379
and Daredevil fight and argue on a rooftop
about what justice the criminal Alfred Coppersmith

00:03:26.379 --> 00:03:27.379
deserves.

00:03:27.379 --> 00:03:31.939
By the way, the Punisher's line to Daredevil
"you're one bad day away from being me" goes

00:03:31.939 --> 00:03:35.830
back to the Daredevil comics and also reminds
me of The Joker's "one bad day" line in the

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:38.040
Batman comic The Killing Joke.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:41.939
And Castle's line to Daredevil that he's a
half measure is a callback to Daredevil's

00:03:41.939 --> 00:03:47.239
first season when Stick criticised Matt for
not going far enough in taking out bad guys.

00:03:47.239 --> 00:03:50.970
When Daredevil asks the Punisher if he's a
Catholic and Frank replies that he used to

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:52.579
be, it's a nod to the What If?

00:03:52.579 --> 00:03:56.299
comic where Castle visits church for confession
and to light candles.

00:03:56.299 --> 00:04:01.019
The dog Frank Castle picks up after massacring
the Irish mob is a reference to the Punisher's

00:04:01.019 --> 00:04:05.540
dog Max in the comics who Castle takes care
of after taking out a drug dealer.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:10.210
And the Irish leader Nesbitt is based on the
Irish mob leader of the same name from The

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:14.569
Punisher volume 6 comics, who gives a similar
rousing speech to his mob associates.

00:04:14.569 --> 00:04:19.720
The Irish mob boss Finn who turns up in episode
4, tortures Castle, and is ultimately blasted

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:24.440
in the face by Castle's gun is likely based
on the comic book character Finn Cooley.

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:28.580
Finn Cooley is an Irish terrorist in The Punisher
Max comics and has a facial disfigurement

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:31.690
after he accidentally set off a bomb in Belfast.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:36.180
Melvin Potter, Daredevil's costume-maker,
returns again for season 2 with new easter

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.750
eggs in his workshop to his origins in the
comics as the villain Gladiator.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:43.810
In the second episode, he gets nervous and
picks up a circular saw, which is a nod to

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:46.000
the weapons Gladiator uses.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.550
Melvin also reveals some body armour in episode
4 which teases a little of the symbol he wears

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:51.570
as Gladiator.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:55.440
The Spanish poster for the film "Revenge of
the Gladiators" is still there from season

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:56.440
1.

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:00.330
And there's a page on his wall with the words
Giant Frogs on it, which is a reference to

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:04.789
the villain Leap-Frog who Gladiator teams
up with alongside Electro to form the group

00:05:04.789 --> 00:05:07.080
Electro's Emissaries of Evil.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:11.070
And by the way, Stilt Man's legs and armour
from the first season are still in Melvin's

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:15.780
workshop which might come in handy for Turk
who just got his leg cut really badly by the

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:16.780
Hand.

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:20.850
In the comics, the small-time crook Turk steals
Stilt Man's legs, which could be a hint that

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.150
we'll see Stilt-Man on the show in the future.

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:27.430
Nobu comes back from the dead this season
suggesting his character is based on the ninja

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:32.110
Kirigi who works for The Hand in the comics
and can recover from death through meditation.

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:35.979
And when Stick slices Nobu's head off after
Daredevil throws him from the roof, it's a

00:05:35.979 --> 00:05:40.490
nod to the way Elektra appears to finish off
Kirigi in the comics by decapitating him.

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:44.400
But interestingly there's a second character
that Nobu could also be based on as it was

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.139
revealed this season that Nobu's full name
is Nobu Yoshioka.

00:05:48.139 --> 00:05:52.979
In the comics, the character of Kagenobu Yoshioka
was instrumental in bringing the Hand together

00:05:52.979 --> 00:05:54.810
in the 16th century.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:59.120
When Nobu kills Elektra with a sai on the
rooftop it feels similar to the way Bullseye

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:01.530
also killed her with a sai in the Daredevil
comics.

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:05.199
And seeing her body dug up again by the Hand
hints at the way she was resurrected by the

00:06:05.199 --> 00:06:09.889
Hand in the comics where the Hand's ninjas
are ordered to give their blood for Elektra.

00:06:09.889 --> 00:06:14.130
Bullseye still hasn't made an appearance on
the TV show but like in season 1 we get another

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:19.000
tease for him in the coffee cups at Metro-General
which have playing cards printed on them.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:21.790
Bullseye often used playing cards as weapons
in the comics.

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:26.840
The coffee cups also look like the Wildcard
Poker coffee cup from Terminator 2.

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:31.979
We also get more hints that Madame Gao may
be based on Crane Mother from the comics.

00:06:31.979 --> 00:06:37.420
In season 1, Gao said her homeland was considerably
farther than China, and this season she stated

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:40.139
that New York is only one of her territories.

00:06:40.139 --> 00:06:44.349
In the comics, Crane Mother rules over one
of the Legendary Cities of Heaven, Ku'n-Zi,

00:06:44.349 --> 00:06:46.539
and she also has links to Iron Fist.

00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:51.349
The opening scene after the first episode's
credits, which shows kids playing by a leaking

00:06:51.349 --> 00:06:55.210
fire hydrant in the street may be a nod to
the The Man Without Fear comics in which a

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:59.379
young Matt Murdock steals a baton from a police
officer who is lecturing a bunch of kids about

00:06:59.379 --> 00:07:01.650
playing with a city fire hydrant.

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:05.490
When Elektra and Matt take off in a fast sports
car, it's a little callback to when Elektra

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:08.910
speeds off in her car with Matt in The Man
Without Fear comics.

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:13.370
The Punisher War Zone comic book series gets
a big nod throughout the season with "war

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:16.550
zone" mentioned multiple times in regards
to The Punisher's activities.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:20.449
Colonel Ray Schoonover, who turns out to be
the Blacksmith smuggling drugs into New York,

00:07:20.449 --> 00:07:24.970
has a similar story in the Punisher War Journal
series in which Schoonover smuggled drugs

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:27.070
out of Vietnam hidden in body bags.

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:30.509
By the way, Blacksmith in the comics is the
name of a Skrull alien.

00:07:30.509 --> 00:07:35.830
And in the episode finale, after Punisher
kills Schoonover, he returns to his own home

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:38.020
to recover a disc with the word Micro on it.

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:43.270
In the comics, Micro is a nickname for David
Linus Lieberman aka "Microchip", a computer

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:46.110
programmer who helps Castle with weapons and
technology.

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:51.560
Pier 81 where Blacksmith's drug shipment comes
in, seems to be a regular criminal haunt in

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:56.200
Daredevil's New York, as it was a Pier 81
warehouse in season 1 where Daredevil went

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:58.919
to find Fisk and ended up fighting Nobu.

00:07:58.919 --> 00:08:02.590
The way the Punisher took out the Mexican
cartel and hung them to die in a meat storage

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:07.150
plant is a nod to how the Punisher used equipment
like meat hooks and grinders or even meat

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:12.900
itself to deal with opponents in the Streets
of Laredo comics, the 1989 and 2004 Punisher

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:17.479
movies, and the 2005 Punisher video game which
features a meat packing plant.

00:08:17.479 --> 00:08:21.340
Like Will Simpson from Jessica Jones, Frank
Castle's military background in the comics

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:23.669
has been updated to serving in Iraq and Afghanistan.

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:27.919
But there's still a nod to Castle's comic
book history in Vietnam when he talks to the

00:08:27.919 --> 00:08:30.380
Vietnam War veteran on the roof in episode
3.

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:34.820
And, as well as being an obvious reference
to his daughter's favourite book and Castle's

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:39.030
feelings of guilt, "one batch, two batch"
might also be a subtle nod to the similarities

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:43.360
between Frank Castle and Will Simpson whose
medication consists of one red pill to get

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:47.080
him going, two white to keep him even and
one blue to bring him down.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:52.190
There's a nod to Wilson Fisk's alter ego of
"Kingpin" when Dutton tells Fisk, "I'm the

00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:53.700
kingpin of this bitch."

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:58.130
And when Dutton's dying in his prison-hospital
bed, Fisk visits him and says, 'In prison,

00:08:58.130 --> 00:09:00.710
there's only room for one kingpin.'

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:05.640
Fisk's lawyer, Mr Donovan, is likely inspired
by the character "Big" Ben Donovan, a Harlem-based

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:09.400
lawyer who appears in the Luke Cage comics;
so there's a good chance we'll see him back

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:11.160
in the Luke Cage series.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:15.910
And just before Punisher escapes from prison
he says to Fisk "next time I see you... only

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:17.180
one of us walks away".

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:21.080
This might be a nod to the PunisherMAX series
where Punisher and Fisk have a brutal, bloody

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:24.620
showdown in which the Punisher finally kills
the Kingpin.

00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:29.110
Blake Tower's comic book counterpart is New
York's district attorney and helps Daredevil

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:30.800
catch various criminals.

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:34.310
In the show, we get to see him help Karen
Page who does most of the detective work this

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:38.740
season and seems to have taken on the role
of Ben Urich who was killed in season 1 by

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:39.740
Fisk.

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:43.840
Page has taken his car and she later takes
his job and desk at the New York Bulletin.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:48.600
Karen Page's past gets explored a little further
this season and we find out she had a brother.

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:52.481
When Karen opens up the file on her in Ben
Urich's old office, there's a glimpse of a

00:09:52.481 --> 00:09:56.830
newspaper cutting with the headline "Mystery
Accident Causes Teen Fatality".

00:09:56.830 --> 00:10:01.170
The whole article isn't visible, but one part
of it reads that a "young man, Kevin Paxton

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:06.130
Page, of Fagan Corners" veered off the road
for an unknown reason and the man was killed.

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:09.500
Further down, there's mention of a little
old man with a grey military moustache limping

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:13.850
away and something about a girl of eight or
nine crying and mention of a brother.

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:17.720
There's no record of Karen Page having a brother
in the comics, however she does have a father

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:22.570
called Paxton Page, a scientist who exposed
himself to too much cobalt radiation and became

00:10:22.570 --> 00:10:24.820
a Daredevil villain called Death's Head.

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:29.040
When Frank says to Karen she looks like she
knows how to use a gun, it's a neat callback

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:32.690
to when Karen shot Fisk's right-hand man Wesley
last season.

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:36.130
And there's another nod to that moment when
one of Foggy and Matt's clients Mr Maxwell,

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:40.150
says "Your girl's a bad-ass!" and Karen replies
"you've no idea."

00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:44.650
There's more hints in season 2 to Claire Dawson's
alter ego of Night Nurse in the comics when

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:47.770
we find out she works the night shift at Metro
General Hospital.

00:10:47.770 --> 00:10:52.020
And there's a reference back to Luke Cage
and Claire's appearance in Jessica Jones when

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:55.970
she talks about getting into trouble after
looking after a big guy at the hospital.

00:10:55.970 --> 00:11:00.211
Jessica Jones herself got a direct mention
when Foggy's ex-girlfriend Marci reveals that

00:11:00.211 --> 00:11:03.650
the super-powered private investigator is
already under scrutiny.

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:08.270
There's also a cool cameo by Carrie-Anne Moss
as Jeri Hogarth, the tough lawyer introduced

00:11:08.270 --> 00:11:09.700
in Jessica Jones.

00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:13.510
And while Foggy was trying to find Claire
at Metro General, one of the nurses mentions

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:18.740
a Mr Carver, which could be a nod to William
Carver who once worked for Foggy in the comics,

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:22.710
and also became Thunderbolt in the Luke Cage
and Iron Fist comics.

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:27.520
When Foggy jokes about a codename for Frank
Castle, he suggests Killdozer or Dumbass With

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:32.370
A Gun, which is a funny link to when Jessica
Jones who taunted Kilgrave about his name!

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:36.920
Killdozer is also the name of a minor Marvel
villain who appeared in a Marvel Super-Heroes

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:38.120
Summer Special.

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:41.860
And when the Dogs of Hell gang member says
to Foggy "You got guts, Harvard" it's a nice

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:45.940
nod to the alias Foggy creates for himself
in the Daredevil comics when he tries to infiltrate

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:47.850
a gang as "Guts" Nelson.

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:52.940
Sgt Mahoney compares the Punisher to Charles
Bronson from the 1974 movie Death Wish which

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:57.480
is about a man who takes up vigilantism after
his wife is killed and his daughter is raped,

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:00.400
a parallel to Frank Castle's personal family
tragedy.

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:04.710
And Mahoney makes the first reference to the
current theme in the MCU and specifically

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:08.620
Captain America: Civil War of whether the
public supports superheroes or whether their

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:09.920
powers should be controlled.

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:13.850
And this theme keeps cropping up throughout
the season, for example, when Brett quips

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:18.180
that someday he's gonna tell Daredevil how
to do his job, a little nod to Civil War's

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:19.770
Sokovia Accords.

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:23.200
Mahoney's foreboding that somebody's going
to get caught in the crossfire sooner or later

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:27.130
likely foreshadows the death of an important
character in Civil War.

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:30.130
And there are plenty of easter eggs to the
rest of the MCU.

00:12:30.130 --> 00:12:35.420
The Roxxon corporation returns, having appeared
previously in season 1 and also the Iron Man

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:37.750
movies, Agent Carter and Agents of SHIELD.

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:42.250
The name of Roxxon boss Hirochi is likely
a nod to Lord Hirochi in the comics who led

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:44.300
a faction of the Hand.

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:47.300
Agents of SHIELD also gets some specific easter
eggs.

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:51.900
The motorcycle club Dogs of Hell appeared
in the first season of SHIELD when they were

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:52.900
mind-controlled.

00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:56.100
By the way, when the Dogs of Hell gang are
hosing down the blood off a stolen truck,

00:12:56.100 --> 00:13:00.390
it's a callback to Daredevil season 1 when
Wilson Fisk's car was being hosed down after

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:03.390
he smashed Anatoly's head in with his car
door.

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:08.140
And the stolen truck here belongs to Redfield
Electronics owned by Jim Redfield who appears

00:13:08.140 --> 00:13:09.810
briefly in the Thunderstrike comics.

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:14.700
There's a flashback moment in episode 3 with
a nun's habit and female voiceover that seems

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:19.060
a likely hint to Matt's mother who in the
comics becomes a nun called Margaret.

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:23.600
And the orphanage, Matt Murdock stayed in
for a time is called St Agnes, the same orphanage

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:26.040
that Skye from Agents of SHIELD grew up in.

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:30.980
And at the New York Bulletin, there's a newspaper
headline "Cybertek Settles", which is a reference

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:34.680
to the tech company that created the cyborg
Deathlok on Agents of SHIELD.

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:39.361
There are also more headlines around the office
such as "Harlem Terror" referring to the Duel

00:13:39.361 --> 00:13:44.080
of Harlem between Hulk and Abomination in
The Incredible Hulk film; "Battle of New York"

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:49.170
referencing the Chitauri invasion in The Avengers;
and when Mitchell Ellison says the newspapers'

00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:53.260
servers were wiped out in the Incident it
could be another connection to the first Avengers

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:57.480
movie or possibly Avengers: Age of Ultron
when Ultron affected the internet.

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:01.630
There's a Star Wars: The Empire Strikes Back
reference when we see a drawing by one of

00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:05.850
Frank Castle's kids of Darth Vader and Luke
Skywalker duelling with lightsabers.

00:14:05.850 --> 00:14:10.680
And the show continues in the tradition of
Marvel's Phase 2's homage to Star Wars limb

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:14.880
loss, with the Irish mobster who loses a hand
when he's gunned down by Castle, and with

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:19.480
Stick cutting off a ninja's hand hand when
he arrives to rescue Daredevil and Elektra.

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:23.500
Matt's romantic moment in the rain with Karen
is a callback to the kiss scene between Ben

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:28.780
Affleck's Daredevil and Jennifer Garner's
Elektra in the critically-panned 2003 Daredevil

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:29.780
movie.

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:33.730
The Yakatomi Building that Elektra and Daredevil
infiltrate feels like a little twist on the

00:14:33.730 --> 00:14:35.930
Nakatomi Plaza from Die Hard.

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:39.340
And Daredevil's corridor and stairway fight
scene with the Dogs of Hell is a call-back

00:14:39.340 --> 00:14:43.390
to Season 1 and draws on the movies Oldboy
and The Raid.

00:14:43.390 --> 00:14:46.730
Foggy's line about the Punisher that "it's
not like our boy was out collecting for the

00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:51.310
Red Cross" is a nod to a similar line Dirty
Harry says about a rapist he shot.

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:55.140
The billboard for Standard Heating Oil, which
appears at the location of the trap Reyes

00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:59.420
set for the Punisher, is a reference to the
film "A Most Violent Year" in which Oscar

00:14:59.420 --> 00:15:04.000
Isaac's character has to deal with truck hijackings,
and it's funny because the Punisher had just

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:06.220
hijacked a truck in the previous episode.

00:15:06.220 --> 00:15:10.150
There's a nod to Michael Mann's movie Manhunter
and Hannibal Lecktor when Daredevil says to

00:15:10.150 --> 00:15:14.360
Castle "I'm never gonna stop coming for you,
until I take you down […] Because you're

00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:18.410
insane" an echo of how Will Graham says to
Hannibal Lecktor that he caught him because

00:15:18.410 --> 00:15:19.490
he's insane.

00:15:19.490 --> 00:15:23.490
Lecktor also tells Will that they're alike,
as Punisher does with Daredevil.

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:26.790
Props to "Fire Power" who suggested this reference
on my Daredevil review!

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:31.610
The Daredevil production crew and some Marvel
artists also get some sneaky shout-outs.

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:35.900
The name Sheila Iniego, who's the show's Assistant
Accountant, is used for the pastor at the

00:15:35.900 --> 00:15:40.160
church where Daredevil faces off against the
masked gunman in the opening episode.

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:45.360
The name Ricky Wex, which Foggy mentions at
the Dogs of Hell club, is a nod to David 'Wex'

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:48.450
Wechsler, one of the show's first assistant
directors.

00:15:48.450 --> 00:15:52.250
The assignment board at the New York Bulletin
has a list of reporters' names that are references

00:15:52.250 --> 00:15:53.390
to more crew.

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:58.230
There's Dave McAllister the key rigging grip,
Alex Foreman a production assistant, cameraman

00:15:58.230 --> 00:16:01.760
Quenell Jones, and set decorator Caroline
Ghertler.

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:07.070
A second version of the board shows set decorator
Stephanie Q. Bowen, stuntman Kevin Michael

00:16:07.070 --> 00:16:11.200
Murphy, and Daredevil: Road Warrior comic
book artist Peter Krause.

00:16:11.200 --> 00:16:15.910
At Fogwell's Gym, there's a poster with Ellison
and Zirlin, nods to Daredevil art assistant

00:16:15.910 --> 00:16:19.510
G. Garrett Ellison and graphics designer Zachary
Zirlin.

00:16:19.510 --> 00:16:22.970
And there's another poster with Little and
Hardenburg, which are references to production

00:16:22.970 --> 00:16:25.450
assistants Ryan Little and Ashlei Hardenburg.

00:16:25.450 --> 00:16:30.260
And the newspaper cutting detailing Paxton
Page's death is written by Tyanna Lazauskas,

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:33.020
who's a construction PA on the Daredevil set.

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:38.650
So, guys, what other Easter Eggs and references
did you spot in Daredevil Season 2?

00:16:38.650 --> 00:16:42.550
Which ones were your favourites and how do
you think this season compared to season 1?

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:45.110
I'm looking forward to reading your comments
below!

